---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gcdCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'gcd(48, 18)?', note: 'The largest integer dividing both. Listing divisors works; Euclid\'s algorithm (Elements, ~300 BCE) is faster and always works.' },
    { math: '48 = 2 × 18 + 12', note: 'Divide and keep the remainder. Any common divisor of 48 and 18 must also divide 12.' },
    { math: '18 = 1 × 12 + 6', note: 'Repeat: the problem shrinks — gcd(18, 12) = gcd(48, 18).' },
    { math: '12 = 2 × 6 + 0 → gcd = 6', note: 'A zero remainder means the last divisor is the answer. Two millennia old, and still the GCD routine inside your computer.' },
  ],
};

const modCfg = {
  title: 'Worked example: the last digit of 7¹⁰⁰, via mod 10',
  steps: [
    { math: 'last digit of N = N mod 10', note: '"mod" means the remainder after division — remainders behave under multiplication.' },
    { math: '7¹ = 7, 7² = 49 ≡ 9, 7³ = 343 ≡ 3, 7⁴ = 2401 ≡ 1 (mod 10)', note: 'the last digits cycle with period 4: 7, 9, 3, 1, 7, 9, 3, 1, …' },
    { math: '100 = 4 × 25 + 0, so 7¹⁰⁰ = (7⁴)²⁵', note: 'the exponent lands exactly on a cycle boundary.' },
    { math: '(7⁴)²⁵ ≡ 1²⁵ = 1 (mod 10)', note: 'each block of four powers multiplies back to last-digit 1.' },
    { math: '∴ 7¹⁰⁰ ends in 1', note: 'no 51-digit number ever computed. Modular arithmetic sees the structure, not the size.' },
  ],
};

const ntToolMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each object with the statement that defines it:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "the division algorithm",
      "right": "a = q·b + r, with unique q and 0 ≤ r < b"
    },
    {
      "left": "gcd(a, b)",
      "right": "the largest integer dividing both"
    },
    {
      "left": "coprime",
      "right": "gcd(a, b) = 1 — the friendly case where inverses exist"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Everything on this page — gcds, clocks, cryptography — is the remainder r taken seriously as a mathematical object in its own right."
};

const ntDigitCloze = {
  "text": "A number is divisible by 9 exactly when its digit sum is, because 10 ≡ %%0%% (mod 9), so 10ᵏ ≡ %%1%% for every power: 4,713 leaves the same remainder as 4 + 7 + 1 + 3 = 15 ≡ %%2%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "1",
      "one"
    ],
    [
      "1",
      "one"
    ],
    [
      "6",
      "six"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Ten is one more than nine; powers inherit it; and 15 mod 9 is…"
};

const ntBezoutSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "gcd(a, b) can always be written as ax + by for integer x and y.",
    "Run the Euclid steps backwards to find x and y.",
    "From 6 = 18 − 12 and 12 = 48 − 2·18 comes 6 = (−1)·48 + 3·18.",
    "gcd(a, b) × lcm(a, b) = a + b."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "gcd(a, b) × lcm(a, b) = a × b — for 48 and 18, 6 × 144 = 864 = 48 × 18.",
  "why": "The identity is behind every 'find the LCM via the GCD' shortcut."
};

const ntPrimesEst = {
  "prompt": "Roughly how many primes are there below 100?",
  "unit": "",
  "answer": 25,
  "factor": 1.2,
  "why": "25 below 100, 168 below 1,000, 9,592 below 100,000 — primes thin out lawfully: the count below n is asymptotically n/ln n.",
  "context": "the prime number theorem was proved in 1896 by Hadamard and de la Vallée Poussin"
};

const ntDensityPlace = {
  "prompt": "Around 10¹⁸, about one number in how many is prime?",
  "labels": [
    "1 in 4",
    "1 in 12",
    "1 in 41.5",
    "1 in 400"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 1,
    "max": 400
  },
  "answer": 41.5,
  "tolerance": 3,
  "why": "About 1 in 41.5 — primes get rarer but never die out, and the average gap near n is ln n."
};

const ntSieveOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild the sieve of Eratosthenes — what comes first:",
  "items": [
    "Write the numbers up to N",
    "Cross out multiples of 2",
    "Cross out multiples of 3, then 5",
    "Any survivor below √N crossing is prime"
  ],
  "why": "To test one large number instead, probabilistic tests like Miller–Rabin do it in milliseconds without factoring anything — the asymmetry cryptography exploits."
};

const ntCancelBranch = {
  "root": "2·3 ≡ 4·3 (mod 6). A student cancels the 3 and concludes 2 ≡ 4 (mod 6). What went wrong?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Diagnose the step:",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Nothing — cancelling is always legal",
          "next": "wrong",
          "feedback": "Cancellation is legal only when the cancelled factor is coprime to the modulus: 3 shares a factor with 6, and the 'division' discards information about multiples of 6."
        },
        {
          "label": "3 is not coprime to 6 — 'dividing' mod m means multiplying by an inverse",
          "next": "right",
          "feedback": "Right — and inverses exist only for coprime factors; mod 5, where 3 is coprime to the modulus, the same cancellation works."
        },
        {
          "label": "The original congruence was false",
          "next": "false",
          "feedback": "It is true — both sides are 0 mod 6; the illegal step is the cancellation."
        }
      ]
    },
    "wrong": {
      "text": "Both sides being 0 hides the difference between 2 and 4."
    },
    "right": {
      "text": "Mod 7, the inverse of 3 is 5 (3 × 5 = 15 ≡ 1), so 'divide by 3' means 'multiply by 5'."
    },
    "false": {
      "text": "The congruence holds; the inference from it does not."
    }
  },
  "why": "Dividing mod m really means multiplying by an inverse — and inverses exist only for coprime factors."
};

const ntFermatMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each modular fact with its instance:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "Fermat's little theorem",
      "right": "a^(p−1) ≡ 1 (mod p) for prime p, a not divisible by p"
    },
    {
      "left": "the inverse of 3 mod 7",
      "right": "5, since 3 × 5 = 15 ≡ 1"
    },
    {
      "left": "clock arithmetic",
      "right": "3 hours after 11 o'clock is 2"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Every nonzero residue's power comes home after p − 1 steps — the engine inside RSA."
};

const ntWilesJudge = {
  "statement": "Fermat's Last Theorem was proven by Andrew Wiles in 1995 using seven chapters of elliptic-curve theory Fermat could not have dreamed of.",
  "answer": true,
  "why": "Conjectured in 1637 in a margin too small for the proof, proven in 1995 — a whole ocean of modern number theory grew from that margin note."
};

const ntRsaOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild the toy RSA computation — what comes first:",
  "items": [
    "Pick primes p = 3, q = 11: n = 33, φ(n) = 20",
    "Publish e = 3, keep d = 7 with e·d ≡ 1 (mod 20)",
    "Encrypt m = 2: c = 2³ mod 33 = 8",
    "Decrypt: 8⁷ mod 33 ≡ 2 — the message returns"
  ],
  "why": "Fermat-and-Euler arithmetic guarantees the answer is m again — every HTTPS padlock is this postscript to Hardy's 'useless' boast."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/number-theory">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Mathematics lesson. It assumes the foundational <em>Ratios,
    Percentages &amp; Proportion</em> lesson — whose "number zoo" sorted the types of number
    and whose powers-and-roots shorthand this page uses constantly — and the intermediate
    <em>Counting &amp; Combinatorics</em> lesson, whose pigeonhole principle is the same
    forced-collision temper of argument you will meet again in Euclid's algorithm. The
    advanced <em>Logic &amp; Proof Techniques</em> lesson houses the contradiction proof this
    page borrows for the infinitude of primes; everything else is built here from division
    with remainders.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>run Euclid's algorithm to compute a gcd, and run it backwards to produce a Bézout combination;</li>
    <li>state the fundamental theorem of arithmetic, and explain what rescues uniqueness — and why 1 is not prime;</li>
    <li>compute in congruences: add and multiply freely, cancel only with coprime factors, and use Fermat's little theorem to tame huge powers;</li>
    <li>solve linear Diophantine equations ax + by = c and write the full family of integer solutions;</li>
    <li>trace RSA end to end with toy numbers, and say precisely what its security does and does not rest on.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every subject in this atlas eventually handles real numbers — measured, approximate,
    continuous. Number theory goes the other way: it studies the <strong>integers</strong>,
    the most discrete objects in mathematics, in all their stubbornness. Which numbers
    divide which? Why do primes sit where they sit? What remains when one integer is
    divided by another? The questions are a child could ask them; the answers took
    millennia — and several are still open. That gap between innocence and difficulty is
    the field's peculiar charm. For centuries number theory was the purest of pure
    mathematics ("beautifully useless", as G. H. Hardy boasted in 1940); since 1977 it has
    been the load-bearing wall of every encrypted transaction on Earth.
  </p>
  <p>
    One tool from the earlier tiers gets promoted to the star of the show: the
    <strong>division algorithm</strong>, the fact that for any integers a and b (b
    positive) there exist <em>unique</em> integers q and r with a = q·b + r and remainder
    r satisfying 0 ≤ r under b. The quotient q is "how many times", the remainder r is
    "what is left". Everything on this page — gcds, clocks, cryptography — is the
    remainder r taken seriously as a mathematical object in its own right.
  </p>
  <Widget no="12" caption="pair each object with its question" widget="match" config={ntToolMatch} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="Euclid's algorithm — the world's oldest surviving algorithm, still in your CPU" widget="equationStep" config={gcdCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards run one computation, but watch what the
    algorithm is really doing: each line replaces the pair with a smaller pair carrying the
    same common divisors, so the gcd never changes while the numbers shrink. Every line is
    the division algorithm a = q·b + r in action, and the remainder r is the star. When the
    remainder hits zero, the last divisor is the answer — and at no point did anyone list
    divisors.
  </p>

  <h2>Divisibility and the greatest common divisor</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>a divides b</strong> (written a | b) when b = a·k for some integer k — no
    remainder. Divisibility is transitive (if a | b and b | c then a | c) and linear
    (divisors of b also divide b's multiples and sums), and these two innocent rules drive
    all the "divisibility tricks". Why does "a number is divisible by 9 exactly when its
    digit sum is"? Because 10 ≡ 1 (mod 9), so 10ᵏ ≡ 1 for every power: 4,713 = 4·1000 +
    7·100 + 1·10 + 3 leaves the same remainder as 4 + 7 + 1 + 3 = 15 ≡ 6. The digit sum is
    the number, mod 9, wearing different clothes — and casting out nines was used for
    centuries to check hand arithmetic long before anyone wrote "mod".
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="fill the blanks — why digit sums detect 9s" widget="cloze" config={ntDigitCloze} />
  <p>
    The <strong>greatest common divisor</strong> gcd(a, b) is the largest integer dividing
    both, and Euclid's algorithm (the widget above) computes it by repeated
    division-with-remainder. It works because any number dividing both a and b also
    divides the remainder a − q·b, and conversely — so the pair (a, b) can be replaced by
    the smaller pair (b, r) without changing the gcd. Each step shrinks the numbers, so
    the process must end, and it ends fast: the number of steps is at most about five
    times the number of digits of the smaller input (a theorem of Lamé, 1844). Two bonus
    facts turn the output into more machinery. First, <strong>Bézout's identity</strong>:
    gcd(a, b) can always be written as ax + by for integer choices of x and y — run the
    Euclid steps backwards: from 6 = 18 − 12 and 12 = 48 − 2·18 comes
    6 = 18 − (48 − 2·18) = 3·18 − 48, so 6 = (−1)·48 + 3·18. Second,
    <strong>gcd(a, b) × lcm(a, b) = a × b</strong>: for 48 and 18, gcd 6 and lcm 144, and
    6 × 144 = 864 = 48 × 18 ✓ — the identity behind "find the LCM via the GCD" shortcuts.
    When gcd(a, b) = 1 the numbers are <strong>coprime</strong>, the friendly case in
    which fractions cancel to lowest terms and, as we will see, modular inverses exist.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the broken identity" widget="spot-error" config={ntBezoutSpot} />

  <h2>Primes and the fundamental theorem</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>prime</strong> is an integer above 1 whose only divisors are 1 and itself;
    everything else above 1 is composite. Primes are the <strong>atoms of
    multiplication</strong>, and the <strong>fundamental theorem of arithmetic</strong>
    says the decomposition is unique: 360 = 2³ × 3² × 5 and no other product of primes
    will ever produce 360. (Uniqueness needs proof — it fails in stranger number systems,
    like the set &#123;a + b√(−5)&#125;, where 6 factors two different ways, 2·3 = 6 =
    (1 + √(−5))·(1 − √(−5)). Euclid's lemma — a prime dividing a product must divide one
    factor — is what rescues uniqueness in the ordinary integers.) Note that
    <strong>1 is not prime</strong>: granting it primality would shatter uniqueness, since
    6 = 2·3 = 1·2·3 = 1·1·2·3 would then all be "different" factorisations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Primes are infinite (the contradiction proof sits in the logic-and-proof lesson) but
    they thin out: 25 below 100, 168 below 1,000, 9592 below 100,000. The <strong>prime
    number theorem</strong> (proved 1896 by Hadamard and de la Vallée Poussin) says the
    thinning is lawful: the count below n is asymptotically n/ln n. Around 10¹⁸, about
    one number in 41.5 is prime — primes get rarer but never die out, and the *average*
    gap near n is ln n. Individual gaps, though, misbehave wildly: twin primes (gap 2)
    are conjectured to appear forever, Yitang Zhang proved in 2013 that <em>some</em> gap
    below 70 million repeats infinitely often (refined since to 246), and nobody knows
    whether every even number is a sum of two primes (Goldbach, 1742). To find all primes
    below a limit, the <strong>sieve of Eratosthenes</strong> remains the practical
    answer: write the numbers, cross out multiples of 2, then 3, then 5 — any survivor
    below √N crossing is prime. To <em>test</em> one large number, smarter probabilistic
    tests (Miller–Rabin) do it in milliseconds without factoring anything — the asymmetry
    that cryptography exploits.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the small-prime census" widget="estimate" config={ntPrimesEst} />
  <Widget no="8" caption="drop the marker — the prime density" widget="place" config={ntDensityPlace} />
  <Widget no="7" caption="rebuild the sieve" widget="order" config={ntSieveOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "prime numbers get rarer and rarer, so eventually they
    must stop." Rarity is real (the density ~1/ln n decays to zero) but Euclid's argument
    guarantees infinity regardless — density zero does not mean emptiness, any more than
    "most numbers are not powers of 2" means powers of 2 run out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> division-with-remainder became an algorithm (Euclid's gcd), the
    algorithm became an identity (Bézout), and coprimality emerged as the friendly case in
    which inverses exist. Next we take factorisation to its limit — the primes as atoms,
    and the uniqueness theorem that makes them the periodic table of multiplication.
  </p>

  <h2>Congruences: arithmetic on a clock</h2>
  <p>
    Write <strong>a ≡ b (mod m)</strong> when a and b leave the same remainder on
    division by m — they differ by a multiple of m. This single definition turns
    remainders into a number system: you can add, subtract and multiply congruences
    freely, because remainders of sums and products depend only on remainders of the
    parts. It is clock arithmetic (3 hours after 11 o'clock is 2), calendar arithmetic
    (what weekday is 1 January 2030?), checksum arithmetic (ISBN and IBAN codes detect
    mistyped digits with one modular equation), and the mathematics of hashing. Division,
    though, is delicate: you may cancel a factor c mod m only when c is coprime to m —
    2·3 ≡ 4·3 (mod 6), both sides being 0, yet 2 ≢ 4 (mod 6). The good case is coprime: mod 7, the inverse of 3 is
    5 (3 × 5 = 15 ≡ 1), so "divide by 3" means "multiply by 5". Fermat's little theorem
    organises this: for prime p and a not divisible by p, <strong>a^(p−1) ≡ 1 (mod p)</strong> —
    every nonzero residue's power comes home after p − 1 steps.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each modular fact with its instance" widget="match" config={ntFermatMatch} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="modular arithmetic answers a 51-digit question in four lines — powers cycle, and cycles have periods" widget="equationStep" config={modCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards never compute a 51-digit number — they track a
    single remainder. Card 2 finds the cycle of last digits by brute force at four small
    powers; card 3 locates the exponent 100 on that cycle; cards 4 and 5 use the cycle's
    structure so that (7⁴)²⁵ collapses to 1²⁵. That is the whole method of modular
    arithmetic: find the period, land the exponent on it, and let structure replace size.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> congruences can be divided through like equations —
  that if 2·3 ≡ 4·3 (mod 6) you may cancel the 3 and conclude 2 ≡ 4 (mod 6). Cancellation
  is legal only when the cancelled factor is coprime to the modulus: 3 shares a factor with
  6, and the "division" actually discards information about multiples of 6. Mod 5, where 3
  is coprime to the modulus, the same cancellation works — which is why "dividing" mod m
  really means multiplying by an inverse, and inverses exist only for coprime factors.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="the illegal cancellation" widget="branch" config={ntCancelBranch} />

  <h2>Diophantine equations: when only integers count</h2>
  <p>
    Many equations are easy over the reals and ferocious over the integers — solutions
    must be whole numbers. The gentlest case, <strong>ax + by = c</strong>, is completely
    settled by Bézout: solutions exist exactly when gcd(a, b) divides c. Example: solve
    12x + 18y = 30. gcd = 6, and 6 | 30, so solutions exist; divide through by 6:
    2x + 3y = 5; by inspection x = 1, y = 1 works; and the full family is
    x = 1 + 3t, y = 1 − 2t for any integer t (each extra x-step of 3 is offset by a
    y-step of −2). This is the mathematics of "buy stamps worth 12¢ and 18¢ to total
    exactly 30¢" — and of scheduling, tile-cutting and coin problems. The flagship hard
    case is <strong>x² + y² = z²</strong>, whose integer solutions are the Pythagorean
    triples (3, 4, 5), (5, 12, 13), (8, 15, 17)… — parametrised completely by
    x = m² − n², y = 2mn, z = m² + n². Raise the exponent and the equation becomes
    <em>xⁿ + yⁿ = zⁿ</em> with no positive integer solutions at all for n above 2 —
    <strong>Fermat's Last Theorem</strong>, conjectured in 1637 in a margin too small for
    the proof, proven by Andrew Wiles in 1995 using seven chapters of elliptic-curve
    theory Fermat could not have dreamed of. A whole ocean of modern number theory
    (elliptic curves, modular forms) grew from that margin note.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — the margin note's fate" widget="judge" config={ntWilesJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> congruences gave arithmetic a clock, and Diophantine equations
    showed remainders constraining whole-number solutions exactly. Next the same machinery —
    coprimality, inverses, Fermat's little theorem — is assembled into the most consequential
    application number theory has ever had: a lock whose key is a pair of primes.
  </p>

  <h2>From pure to critical: RSA</h2>
  <p>
    In 1977 Rivest, Shamir and Adleman built a lock out of Fermat's little theorem and one
    asymmetry: multiplying two large primes is instant; factoring their product back is,
    as far as anyone knows, astronomically hard. The scheme: pick primes p, q; publish
    n = pq and an exponent e coprime to φ(n) = (p − 1)(q − 1); keep d, the number with
    e·d ≡ 1 (mod φ(n)), secret. A message m becomes ciphertext mᵉ mod n; the recipient
    computes (mᵉ)ᵈ mod n, and Fermat-and-Euler arithmetic guarantees the answer is m
    again. Watch it with toy numbers: p = 3, q = 11, n = 33, φ(n) = 20, e = 3, d = 7
    (since 3 × 7 = 21 ≡ 1 mod 20). Message m = 2: cipher c = 2³ mod 33 = 8. Decrypt:
    8⁷ mod 33. Chain the mod-33 reductions: 8² = 64 ≡ 31 ≡ −2, so 8⁴ ≡ 4, and
    8⁷ = 8⁴ · 8² · 8 ≡ 4 · (−2) · 8 = −64 ≡ 2 (mod 33). The message returns. Real keys
    use two primes of 1,024-plus bits each; factoring their 617-digit product is beyond
    all human and machine effort to date — the largest public factorisation record is a
    250-digit number, achieved in 2020 with roughly 2,700 core-years of computation. Every
    HTTPS padlock in your browser is this postscript to Hardy's "useless" boast.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the toy RSA run" widget="order" config={ntRsaOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> RSA's security is a proven theorem. It is not — it rests
    on the <em>belief</em> that factoring is hard, which no one has proved. A fast
    factoring algorithm (or a large enough quantum computer running Shor's algorithm,
    which factors efficiently in principle) would fell the whole edifice overnight; the
    migration to post-quantum cryptographic standards, finalised by NIST in 2024, is the
    industry's hedge against exactly that possibility.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: state Fermat's little theorem precisely, and say where this page used it.</li>
    <li>Application: find the full integer solution family of 12x + 18y = 30, and say why any solution exists at all.</li>
    <li>Why may you cancel a factor of 3 in a congruence mod 7 but not in a congruence mod 6?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) For a prime p and a not divisible by p, a^(p−1) ≡ 1
    (mod p) — every nonzero residue's power comes home after p − 1 steps. It is the engine
    inside the 7¹⁰⁰ example and inside RSA's decryption step. (2) gcd(12, 18) = 6 and 6
    divides 30, so solutions exist; dividing through by 6 gives 2x + 3y = 5, inspection
    finds x = 1, y = 1, and the family is x = 1 + 3t, y = 1 − 2t for any integer t. (3)
    Cancelling c in a congruence mod m means multiplying by an inverse of c, and inverses
    exist exactly when c is coprime to m. Mod 7, 3 is coprime to the modulus (its inverse is
    5); mod 6, 3 shares a factor with the modulus, so the "division" discards information
    about multiples of 6 — 2·3 ≡ 4·3 (mod 6) while 2 ≢ 4 (mod 6).
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The division algorithm a = q·b + r is the engine: gcds, clocks and cryptography are the remainder taken seriously.</li>
    <li>Euclid's algorithm computes the gcd by shrinking pairs; run backwards, it produces Bézout's combination ax + by.</li>
    <li>Primes are the atoms of multiplication, and unique factorisation is a theorem — rescued by Euclid's lemma, broken in stranger number systems.</li>
    <li>A congruence mod m is a number system: add and multiply freely, divide only by factors coprime to m.</li>
    <li>Fermat's little theorem a^(p−1) ≡ 1 (mod p) tames huge powers by finding the cycle.</li>
    <li>Diophantine equations trade easy real solutions for exact integer ones; ax + by = c is solvable exactly when gcd(a, b) divides c.</li>
    <li>RSA multiplies two primes to lock and needs their factorisation to break — security by believed hardness, not by theorem.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lesson <em>Logic &amp; Proof Techniques</em> is this page's toolkit supplier —
    the contradiction argument behind Euclid, induction, and the counterexample discipline all
    live there. The advanced lesson <em>Integration</em> shows the other face of mathematics
    this one defines itself against: continuous accumulation, the setting where ln n — the
    ruler of the prime number theorem — is at home.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Riemann hypothesis.</strong> The prime number theorem's error term is
    governed by the zeros of the Riemann zeta function ζ(s) = Σ 1/nˢ. Riemann (1859)
    conjectured all the interesting zeros lie on the line Re(s) = ½; if true, primes are
    distributed as regularly as possible — the error in the prime count below n is
    bounded by √n·ln n up to constants. It is the most famous open problem in mathematics
    (a Clay Millennium Prize, one million dollars), with over ten trillion zeros computed
    — all on the line — and not one proof. Entire books of number theory begin "assuming
    the Riemann hypothesis…", because so many theorems wait behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Structure inside the chaos.</strong> Deep theorems show primes obey hidden
    patterns. Fermat: an odd prime p is a sum of two squares exactly when p ≡ 1 (mod 4) —
    5 = 1 + 4, 13 = 4 + 9, 17 = 16 + 1, while 3, 7, 11, 19 never decompose; the primes
    know which clock-remainder they were born with. Quadratic reciprocity (Gauss's
    favourite theorem, eight proofs in his lifetime) links "is q a square mod p" to "is p
    a square mod q" with a sign flip depending on the two primes mod 4. Dirichlet's
    theorem guarantees every arithmetic progression a, a + m, a + 2m, … with a, m coprime
    contains infinitely many primes — the primes are fair to every residue class. Each is
    a case of the same meta-phenomenon: locally random, globally lawful.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Records and open frontier.</strong> The largest known primes are Mersenne
    primes 2^p − 1 (the record, found in 2024 by the GIMPS distributed project, is
    2¹³⁶²⁷⁹⁸⁴¹ − 1, a 41-million-digit number; only 52 are known). Perfect numbers —
    equal to the sum of their proper divisors (6 = 1 + 2 + 3) — are tied to Mersenne
    primes by Euclid–Euler: every even perfect number has Euclid's form, and whether an
    <em>odd</em> perfect number exists is open after 2,300 years. The abc conjecture,
    Szemerédi's theorem on arithmetic progressions in the primes (Green–Tao: the primes
    contain progressions of every finite length), and the Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer
    conjecture round out a field where the distance from "state the problem" to "state the
    frontier" is often a single sentence — and two centuries of work.
  </p>

